The camera wearer’s forearm is extended horizontally at arm’s length, directly toward a henna artist seated very close in front of them. The artist is oriented toward and working on the wearer’s hand and forearm, which are fully within the artist’s reach. Other people and objects—legs, fabric, a phone, and a henna cone—remain nearby but outside the wearer’s immediate path. Moving the arm could disrupt or smear the detailed design.

The most appropriate action is to hold still and let the artist finish. This respects the artist’s work and avoids unnecessary interference.

